dict(sec=13, img='C_bio', cap="Serum concentrations of three cardiac markers after myocardial infarction",
stem="A 61-year-old man has recurrent chest pain and new ST elevation in V2–V4 on hospital day 4 after a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction. The time course of three serum markers after a single infarction is shown.{FIG}Which curve and marker would best show that a second infarction has occurred?",
choices=["Curve 1, myoglobin","Curve 2, creatine kinase-MB","Curve 2, troponin","Curve 3, creatine kinase-MB","Curve 3, troponin"], key=1,
exp="Curve 1 rises and falls within hours (myoglobin, now obsolete as a marker). Curve 2 rises at 4–6 hours, peaks about 24 hours and <b>returns to normal in 2–3 days</b>: <b>creatine kinase-MB</b>. Curve 3 rises within 1–3 hours (high-sensitivity assays), peaks at 12–24 hours and <b>stays up for 7–10 days</b>: <b>troponin</b>.<br><br>On day 4 his troponin is still elevated from the first infarct, so a single value cannot show a new event. Creatine kinase-MB (CK-MB) has already normalized, so a <b>new CK-MB rise</b> marks re-infarction — its one remaining niche. In practice, a fresh rise of at least 20% on serial high-sensitivity troponin is now used as well.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> CK-MB normalizes in 2–3 days while troponin stays raised for 7–10 days, which is why CK-MB is classically used to diagnose re-infarction within the first week.",
wrong={0:"Curve 1 is myoglobin; it is too nonspecific and too short-lived to be used.",
2:"Curve 2 is the short-lived marker, CK-MB; troponin is the long-lasting curve 3.",
3:"Curve 3 is the marker that stays up 7–10 days, which is troponin, not CK-MB.",
4:"Troponin (curve 3) is still raised from the first infarct on day 4, so a single value cannot separate old from new injury."},
eli5="Troponin hangs around for a week after a heart attack, so it can't tell you about a second one 4 days later. creatine kinase-MB clears in 2–3 days, so a fresh bump means fresh damage."),

